del can be used to fractionate heterogeneous clinical groups and to find outliers within the heterogeneous cohort. The normative model has been used in schizophrenia, bipolar, and autism spectrum disorders to map the variation within the patient's cohort and address heterogeneity by fractionating cohorts (Baldwin et al., 2022; Bethlehem et al., 2020; Chien et al., 2022; Marquand et al., 2016; Wolfers et al., 2018; Zabihi et al., 2019). Previous studies also confirmed that deviations from the normative  model  were  frequent  in  psychiatric  disorders  but  highly heterogeneous. In the present study, we used the normative modeling approach  that  maps  interindividual  deviations  in  patients  with  firstepisode  drug  naïve  depression  (FEDN-MDD)  in  reference  to  the healthy  range  (see  Fig.  1,  an  overview  of  the  proposed  normative modeling approach showing the steps in the pipeline). Firstly, a slidingtime  window  method  was  used  to  compute  the  dynamic  functional connectivity  matrices  for  each  participant.  Secondly,  subject-specific inherent connectivity networks and corresponding time-varying weight  variability  were  computed  for  each  subject  using  a  group information-guided  independent component analysis technique (GIG-
69:Fig. 1. Data analysis flowchart. (A) Resting-state fMRI data were collected to create dynamic functional connectivity matrices based on AAL atlas using a sliding time window method. (B) Healthy-ICA controls were used to compute the subject-specific networks at the group level, and then these group-level networks were used as guidance information. (C) The back reconstruction algorithm of GIG-ICA was used to compute networks with the time-varying weight of the remaining participants (FEDN-MDD, healthy-base and healthy-comparison individuals), and then the FC-dynamic and network-dynamic characteristics were calculated for each individual. (D) For the characteristics of healthy-base controls, the normative models were estimated between any characteristics and age to identify the normative distribution. (E)  The deviations of each FEDN-MDD and healthy-comparison individual were measured based on the normative models from step (D). (F) Comparison was performed on deviations across groups.
75:ICA). Then, the FC-dynamic (dynamic of functional connectivity) characteristics and network fluctuation characteristics were calculated based on these connectivity networks. Thirdly, quantile polynomial regression was used to build the normative model based on age and each dynamic characteristic in a large healthy cohort to identify the normative range. Finally, we positioned each patient with FEDN-MDD and healthy participants within the normative ranges to estimate the deviation in individual  cases  regarding  these  dynamic  characteristics.  Through  this approach, we expected to 1) map the dynamic characteristics by which each FEDN-MDD participant differs from the expected functional connectivity patterns, and thereby; 2) demonstrate the value of normative modeling techniques for understanding the biological heterogeneity of MDD. The present study will provide a quantitative estimate of the degree of heterogeneity, in terms of the dynamic features (network fluctuation characteristics and FC-dynamic  characteristic) deviations, related to FEDN-MDD.
81:We employed data from International Big-Data Center for Depression Research,  and  the  data  were  collected  from  multiple  hospitals/institutions in China (http://yanlab.psych.ac.cn/IBCDR). Full details on the datasets and preprocessing pipeline have been described previously (Yan et al., 2019). According to the information provided by IBCDR, we selected  subjects  for  group  statistical  analyses  from  1276  MDDs  and 1104  healthy  controls  through  the  following  criteria:  1)  FEDN-MDD aged from 18 to 50 were included, resulting in 276 FEDN-MDDs; 2) healthy controls with age in a range of 18 to 50 were included, resulting in 847 controls; 3) subjects with excessive head motion (mean FD &gt; 0.5 mm or max FD &gt; 2 mm) were excluded, resulting in 274 FEDN-MDDs and 832 controls; 4) 125 healthy control individuals (71 females, as a healthy-ICA group) of 832 controls were included to create the group level  components using GIG-ICA; 5) Another 289 healthy control individuals (165 female, as a healthy-base group) of 832 controls were included to establish a normative model; 6) 274 FEDN-MDD individuals (174 males) and the rest 418 healthy control individuals (232 males, as a healthy-comparison group) recruited from the same study sites were included to conduct statistical analyses. We divided the healthy controls into  three  groups  to  avoid  reusing  data  in  different  steps  of  analysis which might lead to biased results. There were no significant differences between the depression and healthy comparison control cohorts in sex and head motions (see Table 1). However, we detect a slightly older age (31.17y vs 30.13y) and a lower educational level (11.85y vs 14.23y) in the patient group.
106:For each subject, we computed whole-brain time-varying connectivity  matrices  based  on m (m = 90)  ROIs  from  the  automated anatomical  labeling  (AAL)  template  (Tzourio-Mazoyer  et  al.,  2002) using a sliding time window method (window length = 60s) (Allen et al., 2014; Du et al., 2017) as dynamic functional connectivity patterns. For different  sites  with  different  TR  and  time-series  lengths,  the  timevarying  dynamic  connectivity  patterns  represented  by  a  window-byconnectivity matrix included the same length of connectivity strengths ( m × ( m GLYPH&lt;0&gt; 1) / 2) and different numbers of the windows.
134:To assess generalization, we applied 10-fold cross-validation to build an aggregation normative model (Zabihi et al., 2019). We repeatedly trained the polynomial regression model on 90 % of the 289 healthybase  participants  at  different  percentiles,  withholding  the  remaining 10  %  for  estimating  generalization  performance.  This  10-fold  crossvalidation  procedure  yielded  10  normative  models.  Each  of  these models generated a normative distribution based on quantile regressions with 95 % confidence intervals.
136:Having the aggregation normative model based on age and dynamic (network  fluctuation  characteristic  and  FC-dynamic  characteristic) characteristics,  we  next  positioned  each  FEDN-MDD  patient  and healthy-comparison participant within the normative range for healthybase adults of the same age and sex. Thus, each FEDN-MDD patient and the healthy-comparison participant was given 10 individual z scores. We adopted a certainty measure to evaluate the deviation reliability for each subject based on the 10-fold cross-validation normative model. Particularly, the certainty was measured by nz/n, where nz  is the number of nonzeroes  in  these  10 z scores,  and  n  is  the  total  number  of  crossvalidation models (here n = 10). A higher value indicated higher deviation reliability, and vice versa .  Subjects with higher certainty ( ≥ 0.8) were identified as infra- or supra-normal, and their robust z was the average of the nonzeroes z scores.
182:4.07 % of healthy controls were identified as extreme individuals. The distribution of patients and healthy controls on FC-dynamic characteristics was comparable. However, the individuals with more deviations come from the patient's cohort, as in Fig. 4A, and there are 4.74 % of the patients showed deviation in at least 100 connectivities, and only 3.35 % of the controls deviate from the normative model in at least 100 connectivities. These results revealed that more patients showed extreme dynamic variabilities in 100 connectivities. Then we modeled male and female separately due to sex effects in MDD (Fig. 3B). In the top 5 % deviating females, there were 6.32 % of the patients, and 4.13 % of the controls. In the top 5 % deviating males, there were 7 % of the patients, and 3.98 % of the controls. The distribution of the male and female participants with normative deviations was comparable across the two groups. We also investigated sex differences within the patient and HC groups. The FEDN group showed significant sex differences in IC 10 (the left  frontoparietal  network) on  network fluctuation characteristic deviations,  more  male  patients  showed  deviations  than  females  ( p = 0.006). For details on the sex differences within each group, please see the Supplementary Materials.
196:We also analyzed the static functional connectivity differences between the two groups. According to the normative model, the ' outliers ' were identified as the top 5 % of the number of deviations for all connectivity in different groups. To guard the results against the influence of extreme individuals. We excluded them and conducted a second casecontrol  analysis.  Notably,  we  detected  fewer  group  differences  in several inherent connectivity states after excluding the ' outliers ' , which suggested that these disappeared group differences were substantially affected by the ' outliers ' . The results remind us that the identified difference in previous studies may be caused by the small subset patients, and cannot represent the majority (for details, see the supplementary materials).
200:In this study, we mapped the biological heterogeneity of the patients with first-episode drug naïve depression, based on independent component  analysis  derived  brain  measures,  mainly  focused  on  the network-level and FC-level dynamic characteristics in different inherent networks. The individuals with more deviations came from the patient's cohort when the FC-dynamic characteristics were analyzed. The percentage of individuals whose network-dynamic characteristics deviated from the normative model showed a significant difference in DMN, left ECN, and limbic circuits. And the significant differences mainly come from the male patients.
212:left-ECN  network,  specifically,  increased  functional  connectivity  between  the  angular  gyrus,  superior  temporal  gyrus,  and  between  the insula  and  superior  frontal  gyrus.  Plenty  of  studies  have  revealed decreased  functional  connectivity  in  default  mode  networks  (Bluhm et al., 2010; Schultz et al., 2017), as others found the opposite results (Posner et al., 2016). A recent study reported that the FC within the DMN was also positively related to symptom severity(Yan et al., 2019), and another study revealed that the higher severity of depression was related to increased dynamic FC between the medial and dorsolateral prefrontal cortex within the default network (Kaiser et al., 2016). We found decreased functional connectivity between the prefrontal gyrus and occipital cortex and increased functional connectivity between the anterior  cingulate  and  lingual  gyrus  in  FEDN-MDD  patients,  even excluding the ' outliers ' .  However, in the present study, we failed to detect the relationships between abnormal functional connectivity with the severity of depressive symptoms. We believed this was confined by the limited measurements of symptoms. Published studies tried to use the identified neural signatures from case-control studies to classify the patients with MDD from control groups (Formisano et al., 2008; Gupta and Vig, 2019). However, some classification models either failed to distinguish patients from controls effectively or with limited representations,  evidenced  by  the  lower  generalization  accuracy  for  an  independent validation dataset. Our results suggested that the potential of biomarker  identification  was  likely  smaller  than  anticipated,  considering  the  vast  individual  differences,  almost  half  of  the  patients  are deviated on at least one brain network. This individual viability cannot collapse into one or two robust biological subtypes.
216:Ioannides, 2007) was getting more interest, since it provided unique information about communication among neural systems beyond that provided by static functional connectivity (Damaraju et al., 2014). In the present study, we used the sliding time windows correlation method (Allen et al., 2014; Tian et al., 2018; Yue et al., 2018), combined with the normative model, to analyze the distribution of the patients on the FCdynamic  characteristic  within  different  networks.  We  found  that compared to controls, more FEDN-MDD patients were identified as deviations, especially as supra-deviations. This result was consistent with previous  studies,  which  suggested  that  the  communications  between different brain areas were more unstable in MDD patients (Long et al., 2020). Kaiser et al. reported increased fluctuation in functional connectivity between MPFC and insula, and higher severity of depression related  to  increased  dynamic  functional  connectivity(Kaiser  et  al., 2016). Furthermore, researchers have also demonstrated that a combination of temporal and spectral characteristics of brain connectivity has the highest predictive accuracy in discriminating temporal lobe epilepsy patients from healthy controls (Chiang et al., 2018). These results suggested that the dynamic FC approach might represent more accurately the fluctuation nature of the brain. Indeed, we found that the distributions of the FEDN-MDD patients and controls were comparable when considering the functional connectivity as static and constant over the length of the scan.
218:The  combination  of  normative  model  with  functional  fluctuation might facilitate  the  future  study  in  identifying  the  more  represented dynamic biomarker for MDD. However, our findings should be considered in light of several limitations. First, the normative model describes the functional network trajectories along with age, which were based on cross-sectional data and should be validated in a longitudinal cohort.
226:In  conclusion, we estimated a normative model of dynamic functional connectivities in different brain inherent networks, based on a large MDD cohort. We found that the distributions of FEDN-MDD and controls showed more difference when considering the dynamic characteristics compared to static features in functional connectivities. Our results showed that it was necessary to look at the limitation of the casecontrol  paradigm -which collapses individual  patients  with  different neural signatures and even symptoms into one ' average MDD participant ' ,  and  further  understanding  the  heterogeneous  neuroimaging mechanism of MDD. Normative modeling might provide an excellent tool for understanding the heterogeneity of psychiatric disorders, and can be used as complementary to classical case-control comparisons, and this  study  also  posed  a  warning  for  the  limited  potential  of  the biomarkers in psychiatric disorders.
